{
  "meta": {
    "title": "Nutrient Pulse: Diatoms of the California Upwelling Front",
    "date": "2026-08-19",
    "lesson": "When equatorward winds blow along the California coast, Ekman transport pushes surface water offshore and cold, nutrient-rich water wells up from ~100-200m to replace it. Within days, silica- and nitrate-starved diatoms like Chaetoceros and Skeletonema explode into dense chains, riding the front as it sharpens against warmer offshore water. Their exudates feed a fast-turnover copiotroph community (Alteromonas-like heterotrophs) that races phage and grazers for the bloom's carbon before the front relaxes and the bloom collapses. This level asks you to found a colony at the edge of a moving nutrient front: chase the upwelled plume, digest diatom-derived carbohydrate and protein exudate, and survive the grazer and phage pressure that a rich, ephemeral bloom always attracts.",
    "citation": "Based on classic CalCOFI/California Current upwelling-front diatom bloom dynamics (e.g. Chaetoceros/Skeletonema blooms, Kudela & Chavez studies)",
    "difficulty": "normal",
    "realWorldBasis": "California Current System coastal upwelling front, spring/summer diatom bloom succession"
  },
